e scientific information more reproducible and reusable, and promote the integration of computers in scientific reasoning. We believe the greater automation of both the physical and intellectual aspects of scientific investigations to be essential to the future of science. Greater automation improves the accuracy and reliability of experiments, increases the pace of discovery and, in common with conventional laboratory automation, removes tedious and repetitive tasks from the human scientist."}]", "excerpt_truncated": true, "source_sha256": "cf9b1bc4518239fd6644b0e9f5b7b992e40fe5954b38ddca6cb08b55c368e1d1", "verification_required": true, "topic_domain": "unassigned_problem", "evidence_role": "discovery", "host_tier": "discovery", "source_identity": null, "persistent_identifiers": ["doi:10.48550/arxiv.2508.14111", "doi:10.1186/1759-4499-2-1.pdf", "doi:10.1186/1759-4499-2-1", "openalex:W4415238177", "arxiv:2508.14111"], "source_candidates": ["https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2508.14111", "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2508.14111", "https://doi.org/10.1186/1759-4499-2-1", "https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1186/1759-4499-2-1.pdf"], "source_candidate_identities": {}}
